  "answer": "**Final status: passing.** It took two runs: the first run failed and the run after the fix passed.\n\n- `make build`: 0 errors. The warnings are only NuGet vulnerability advisories, unrelated to this.\n- `make unit`: 272 of 272 passed, on both target frameworks.\n- `make component`: 20 of 20 passed.\n\n**What failed:** one unit test, `DeltaFormatterTests.Format_FieldInActualNotInExpected_ShowsAbsentSentinel`. It expected the failure output to contain `<absent>`, but got `expected: <not present>`.\n\n**Root cause:** `src/ConfIT/Matching/DeltaFormatter.cs:69`. When the response has a field the expected body doesn't, the formatter printed `<not present>` as the placeholder. Everything else in the repo says `<absent>`: `doc/failure-output.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, and the skills' `writing-tests.md` troubleshooting tables, which tell users to look for `expected: <absent>`. So the code was wrong, not the test.\n\n**What was changed:** that one line in production code, `TestColor.Subtle(\"<not present>\")` \u2192 `TestColor.Subtle(\"<absent>\")`. No tests were changed and nothing was committed.",
